<section class="three-tool-knowledge" id="tawaf-deep-guide"><h2>Tawaf and Sa'i Counter: seven circuits, Safa to Marwah and complete guide</h2><p><strong>Short answer:</strong> Tawaf generally comprises <strong>seven complete circuits of the Kaaba</strong>, while Sa'i consists of <strong>seven one-way lengths between Safa and Marwah</strong>. The first Sa'i length is Safa to Marwah, the second returns to Safa, and the seventh ends at Marwah. Separate saved counters aid attention but cannot determine ritual validity.</p><nav class="three-tool-nav" aria-label="Guide sections"><a href="#tawaf-deep-difference">Tawaf versus Sa'i</a><a href="#tawaf-deep-tawaf">How to count seven Tawaf </a><a href="#tawaf-deep-sai">Sa'i route</a><a href="#tawaf-deep-steps">How to use the separate T</a><a href="#tawaf-deep-example">Worked example</a><a href="#tawaf-deep-sources">Sources</a></nav><div class="three-tool-facts"><div><strong>Tawaf: seven circuits</strong></div><div><strong>Sa'i: seven lengths</strong></div><div><strong>Progress: private browser</strong></div></div><section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-difference"><h3>Tawaf versus Sa'i: what exactly is being counted?</h3><p>Tawaf is circumambulation of the Kaaba, with each complete circuit conventionally counted when returning to the Black Stone starting line. Sa'i is a distinct rite between the hillocks of Safa and Marwah. Quran 2:158 mentions Safa and Marwah, and Sahih Muslim 1218a gives a substantial description of the Prophet's pilgrimage rites.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-tawaf"><h3>How to count seven Tawaf circuits</h3><p>Begin according to the appropriate rite and follow a qualified guide's instructions for intention, starting location and movements. Count one only on completing an entire circuit back to the starting marker. Continue to seven, using Undo if a tap was accidental. The app deliberately does not certify purity, physical direction, crowd restrictions or whether a circuit met the necessary requirements.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-sai"><h3>Sa'i route: Safa to Marwah for seven one-way lengths</h3><p>Length one starts at Safa and ends at Marwah. Length two travels from Marwah to Safa, and so on; odd-numbered lengths end at Marwah. After seven one-way lengths you finish at Marwah. Seven round trips would be fourteen lengths and is not the conventional count for Sa'i. The counter displays the next direction based on saved progress.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-steps"><h3>How to use the separate Tawaf and Sa'i counters</h3><p>Open the tool before starting the ritual. Increment Tawaf only after a completed circuit and Sa'i only after a completed one-way length. The counters stop at seven. Undo an accidental increment or confirm before a reset. Download a private snapshot if useful for continuity, while understanding that the snapshot does not prove the rites were performed.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-example"><h3>Worked example: your third Sa'i length</h3><p>Suppose the saved Sa'i counter reads two. The first length finished at Marwah and the second returned to Safa, so the next displayed direction is <strong>Safa to Marwah</strong>. After recording the third length, the direction switches to Marwah to Safa. Once the seventh is recorded, the tool says the route finishes at Marwah.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-ritual"><h3>Important religious conditions the counter cannot check</h3><p>The counter has no camera, location proof or ritual-verification mechanism. It cannot assess whether Tawaf was performed correctly, whether a break invalidated or changed a rite, what should be done after doubt, or whether special circumstances require a different treatment. Seek a qualified scholar or guide for such questions.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-safety"><h3>Crowd safety and accessible pilgrimage</h3><p>Use the counter only when it does not distract from walking, crowd awareness or personal safety. Travellers with mobility needs should follow appropriate accessible routes and authorised staff guidance. A device can be put away; safeguarding physical health and safe movement is more important than preserving a digital tally.</p></section>
<section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-privacy"><h3>Saved rounds and the progress snapshot</h3><p>The existing browser storage key for Tawaf and Sa'i remains unchanged after the upgrade. Your two numeric counters are saved locally; a downloaded JSON snapshot contains only the count and version. Clearing browser storage or changing devices can remove progress. The snapshot is not a Hajj or Umrah completion certificate.</p></section><section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-faq"><h3>Frequently asked questions</h3>{faqs.map(([q,a])=><details class="faq-detail"><summary>{q}</summary><p>{a}</p></details>)}</section><section class="three-tool-section" id="tawaf-deep-sources"><h3>Sources and further reading</h3><ul><li><a href="https://quran.com/2/158" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Quran 2:158</a></li><li><a href="https://sunnah.com/muslim:1218a" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Sahih Muslim 1218a</a></li><li><a href="https://umrah.nusuk.sa/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">Official Nusuk Umrah</a></li></ul><p class="small">Original educational material. An ingredient list, travel checklist or counter is not religious certification. Independent qualified scholarly review remains pending.</p></section></section>